        <p>Every week someone walks into one of our stores with a guest list, a date, and a look of mild panic. Good news: the math is easy, and we've done it for enough backyard birthdays, rehearsal dinners, and full-on weddings to know where it goes wrong. Here's the simple version, plus a table you can screenshot.</p>

        <h2>The One Rule to Remember</h2>
        <p>Plan on <strong>one drink per guest per hour</strong>, and budget a bit more (call it two) for the first hour, when everyone arrives thirsty and nobody's eating yet. For a typical 4-hour party, that's about <strong>5 drinks per guest</strong>. Nobody drinks exactly that, but across a crowd it averages out surprisingly well.</p>
        <p>Then adjust for who's coming. A 4-hour wedding reception with a twenty-something crowd and a dance floor can run higher; a family lunch with a lot of kids and grandparents runs lower. If half your guest list doesn't drink, count them out and add a great non-alcoholic option. And always round up. Running out is a story people tell; leftovers are next weekend's party.</p>

