        <p>For wine, lean toward white and sparkling in warm months and red in winter. For spirits, four bottles usually means vodka, tequila, bourbon, and gin; two bottles means vodka and tequila (this is Austin). Our <a href="/guides/classic-cocktails-at-home.html">classic cocktails guide</a> and <a href="/guides/ranch-water-guide.html">ranch water guide</a> can help you pick a signature drink instead of stocking a full bar.</p>

        <h2>Mixers, Ice, and the Stuff People Forget</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Mixers:</strong> figure about 3 parts mixer to 1 part spirit by volume, so roughly 2 liters of mixer for every 750 ml bottle of spirits. Topo Chico, club soda, tonic, and a citrus soda cover almost everything.</li>
          <li><strong>Ice:</strong> about <strong>1 pound per guest</strong> for a 4-hour party, and closer to 2 pounds if it's outdoors in a Texas summer or you're chilling bottles in tubs. A 20-pound bag serves about 15 to 20 people.</li>
          <li><strong>Citrus:</strong> one lime or lemon per 4 cocktails. Cut them right before the party.</li>
          <li><strong>Non-alcoholic:</strong> at least one interesting option (sparkling water with fruit, a mocktail base) at roughly one drink per hour for guests who aren't drinking, plus water for everyone.</li>
          <li><strong>Cups and glassware:</strong> plan 2 to 3 per guest, because people set them down and forget.</li>
        </ul>

